            <ArticleTitle>A case of portomesenteric venous gas detected on computed tomography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>517-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2478/pjs-2013-0080</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">/j/pjs.2013.85.issue-9/pjs-2013-0080/pjs-2013-0080.xml</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Portomesenteric vein gas is a rare condition, which pathogenesis is not completly understood. One of causes is e.g. mesenteric ischemia. Pathogenesis of this condition are: intraabdominal sepsis, interventional procedures, liver transplantation, Crohn disease and trauma. In 15% of causes its idiopathic. Hepatic portal venous gas predict high risk of mortality (&gt;50%). An advanced radiology techniques such as computed tomography can be helpful in recognizing of this pathology stage. We want to report a case of 83-year-old man with acute abdominal pain after cardiovascular procedure, with portomesenteric vein gas and bowel pneumatosis detected on computed tomography.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
